1f\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"The plain (non-outcome) av lane: `av-opts {:convention :arg-vector :native (set (keys vm/primitives))}` and `av-modules` = default registry + stream + cell + data. Under non-outcome convention, the arity else branch is `(data/count dsg__av)` which the data module refuses with :reason :arity — the payload is the data module's refusal, not the arity-error map. The arity-error map (with :yin.k/fn etc.) only appears with `:outcomes true`. The outcome runner (`run-outcome`) uses outcome-modules = av-modules + 'portable host module with outcome-adapted nth + 'probe. And `outcome-opts {:convention :arg-vector, :outcomes true, :native (set (keys vm/primitives)), :adapters {'data/nth 'portable/nth}}`.\\\\n\\\\nWait — but the cohort's unit-opts uses `:adapters library-adapters` and `:native vm/primitive-profiles`. For MY payload-values test I can use a local copy of outcome-opts/outcome-modules (modeled on clojure_test's, portable). Does `run-outcome` work on all hosts? It's in clojure_test.clj (JVM-only) but uses only portable pieces (tu/, yang/outcome-adapter, vm/primitive-profile, module/register-host-module). The callsite test runs on all 4 hosts — does IT use register-host-module anywhere... The r2 report says the callsite battery runs 40 tests on Node/Dart. I'll model my namespace's requires on the callsite test's (which is proven portable on all four hosts): requires clojure.test, dao.jing, yang.clojure, yang.clojure.prelude?, yang.core.call?, yin.vm, yin.vm.encoder, yin.vm.module, yin.vm.data, yin.vm.macro, yin.vm.semantic.linearize, yin.vm.semantic.derive, yin.vm.test-utils?, dao.jing.mem, yin.vm.linker.publish...\\\\n\\\\nWait — is `yin.vm.test-utils` portable to Dart/Jolt? It requires dao.stream, dao.stream.memory-log, dao.stream.ringbuffer, dao.stream.observer — cljc files. Are they in the cljd aggregation (bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only)? The Dart lane compiles the namespace + deps. dao.stream.ringbuffer on Dart — dart datagram quirks memory suggests dao.stream works on Dart. The callsite test itself requires `#?@(:clj [[dao.stream :as stream] [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]])` — gated to :clj! So dao.stream IS problematic on non-JVM hosts?? But test-utils requires it ungated... and clojure_test.clj is JVM-only anyway. Hmm — do any PORTABLE (4-host) tests require yin.vm.test-utils? yang.core.call-test — is IT in the J2 included list? No (not listed). Does call_test run on Dart? Let me check cljd_agg to see how namespaces are selected and whether test-utils is Dart-clean.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"93169effb67a4e4998a244e0\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"51f76b76-407e-4792-b3c6-0944d15f3315\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:25:09.950Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":8736,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:21896:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_1a726615c62e4d02b3a4c9c4\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"(ns cljd-agg\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Dart test runner that aggregates the generated test files.\\\\n\\\\n   `flutter test` loads every generated *_test.dart separately (about 7.5 s\\\\n   each). This runner compiles the test namespaces through the cljd tool,\\\\n   groups the generated files into a few shard files that import them and call\\\\n   each `main`, and runs `flutter test` on the shards only. Shards live under\\\\n   build/, never under test/, so a plain `cljd test` never runs them twice.\\\\n\\\\n   Run with babashka:\\\\n     bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj [--slow-only | --slow-regex | --only NS,NS...]\\\\n   --slow-only restricts the run to the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard.\\\\n   A namespace in that set with no generated Dart file is fatal.\\\\n   --only compiles and runs only the given test namespaces (bb test:changed,\\\\n   bb test:sub); one with no generated Dart file is fatal too, EXCEPT a\\\\n   JVM-only namespace: one whose every deftest sits behind a reader\\\\n   conditional that excludes ClojureDart (for example\\\\n   dao.data.sorted-user-type-test). The cljd tool compiles such a namespace as\\\\n   a plain library (lib/cljd-out/<ns>.dart exists) and emits no test file;\\\\n   that is reported and skipped, never fatal. A namespace that produced\\\\n   neither file is still fatal.\\\\n   --slow-regex prints, for bb.edn, an anchored alternation of those\\\\n   namespaces with its dots already escaped for embedding in an EDN string\\\\n   (shadow-cljs --config-merge); it is not a ready-to-use regex. It exits 1\\\\n   when no namespace uses the guard, so a slow lane cannot be vacuous.\\\\n   DATOM_SLOW_TESTS is inherited from the environment.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  (:require\\\\n    [babashka.fs :as fs]\\\\n    [babashka.process :as p]\\\\n    [clojure.string :as str]))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(def test-root \\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\")\\\\n(def out-root \\\\\\\"test/cljd-out\\\\\\\")\\\\n(def shard-dir \\\\\\\"build/cljd-agg\\\\\\\")\\\\n(def max-shards 8)\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- ns-of\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The namespace declared by the first ns form of `file`.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  [file]\\\\n  (second (re-find #\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\(ns\\\\\\\\s+\\\\\\\\^?(?:\\\\\\\\{[^}]*\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\s+)?([^\\\\\\\\s\\\\\\\\)]+)\\\\\\\" (slurp file))))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- source-files\\\\n  []\\\\n  (->> (file-seq (fs/file test-root))\\\\n       (filter #(re-find #\\\\\\\"_test\\\\\\\\.clj[cd]$\\\\\\\" (str %)))\\\\n       (remove #(str/includes? (str %) \\\\\\\"cljd-out\\\\\\\"))\\\\n       (map str)\\\\n       sort))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- test-namespaces\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Distinct: a namespace with both a .cljc and a .cljd source (for example\\\\n   dao.stream.cbor-test) has one generated Dart file, which must run once.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  []\\\\n  (distinct (keep ns-of (source-files))))\\\",\\\"is_error\\\":false}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"76a72f16-b2eb-44b4-b89d-6b84c6ebae69\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:25:10.182Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"(ns cljd-agg\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Dart test runner that aggregates the generated test files.\\\\n\\\\n   `flutter test` loads every generated *_test.dart separately (about 7.5 s\\\\n   each). This runner compiles the test namespaces through the cljd tool,\\\\n   groups the generated files into a few shard files that import them and call\\\\n   each `main`, and runs `flutter test` on the shards only. Shards live under\\\\n   build/, never under test/, so a plain `cljd test` never runs them twice.\\\\n\\\\n   Run with babashka:\\\\n     bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj [--slow-only | --slow-regex | --only NS,NS...]\\\\n   --slow-only restricts the run to the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard.\\\\n   A namespace in that set with no generated Dart file is fatal.\\\\n   --only compiles and runs only the given test namespaces (bb test:changed,\\\\n   bb test:sub); one with no generated Dart file is fatal too, EXCEPT a\\\\n   JVM-only namespace: one whose every deftest sits behind a reader\\\\n   conditional that excludes ClojureDart (for example\\\\n   dao.data.sorted-user-type-test). The cljd tool compiles such a namespace as\\\\n   a plain library (lib/cljd-out/<ns>.dart exists) and emits no test file;\\\\n   that is reported and skipped, never fatal. A namespace that produced\\\\n   neither file is still fatal.\\\\n   --slow-regex prints, for bb.edn, an anchored alternation of those\\\\n   namespaces with its dots already escaped for embedding in an EDN string\\\\n   (shadow-cljs --config-merge); it is not a ready-to-use regex. It exits 1\\\\n   when no namespace uses the guard, so a slow lane cannot be vacuous.\\\\n   DATOM_SLOW_TESTS is inherited from the environment.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  (:require\\\\n    [babashka.fs :as fs]\\\\n    [babashka.process :as p]\\\\n    [clojure.string :as str]))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(def test-root \\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\")\\\\n(def out-root \\\\\\\"test/cljd-out\\\\\\\")\\\\n(def shard-dir \\\\\\\"build/cljd-agg\\\\\\\")\\\\n(def max-shards 8)\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- ns-of\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The namespace declared by the first ns form of `file`.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  [file]\\\\n  (second (re-find #\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\(ns\\\\\\\\s+\\\\\\\\^?(?:\\\\\\\\{[^}]*\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\s+)?([^\\\\\\\\s\\\\\\\\)]+)\\\\\\\" (slurp file))))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- source-files\\\\n  []\\\\n  (->> (file-seq (fs/file test-root))\\\\n       (filter #(re-find #\\\\\\\"_test\\\\\\\\.clj[cd]$\\\\\\\" (str %)))\\\\n       (remove #(str/includes? (str %) \\\\\\\"cljd-out\\\\\\\"))\\\\n       (map str)\\\\n       sort))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- test-namespaces\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Distinct: a namespace with both a .cljc and a .cljd source (for example\\\\n   dao.stream.cbor-test) has one generated Dart file, which must run once.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  []\\\\n  (distinct (keep ns-of (source-files))))\\\",\\\"stderr\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"interrupted\\\":false,\\\"isImage\\\":false,\\\"noOutputExpected\\\":false},\\\"tool_result_meta\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_1a726615c62e4d02b3a4c9c4\\\",\\\"permission_decision\\\":{\\\"decision\\\":\\\"accept\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"config\\\",\\\"reason_type\\\":\\\"subcommandResults\\\"}}]}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:22259:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_202611190025104ddb34b7737d46b8\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"The Dart lane compiles ALL test namespaces through cljd and runs selected ones. So my namespace must be Dart-compilable (all requires must be cljc-loadable under :cljd). The callsite test requires: clojure.test, clojure.string, clojure.walk, dao.jing, dao.jing.cbor, dao.jing.mem, dao.space.index, dao.space.query, (clj-only: dao.stream, ringbuffer), yang.clojure, yang.clojure.prelude, yang.core.call, yang.frontend, yang.safepoint, yang.tails, yin.repl, yin.repl.frontends, yin.repl.store, yin.shell.query, yin.vm, yin.vm.data, yin.vm.encoder, yin.vm.linker.closure, dao.stream.apply, yin.repl.query, yin.vm.linker.dht, (clj: interpreter), yin.vm.linker.publish, yin.vm.macro, yin.vm.module, yin.vm.semantic.linearize, yin.vm.semantic.derive.\\\\n\\\\nNo yin.vm.test-utils, no dao.stream.ringbuffer (clj-gated), no ast-walker. Hmm. So for the four-lane runners, is there a portable precedent? yang.core.call-test requires test-utils + ast-walker + debruijn runtimes — is it Dart-clean? Let me check whether yang.core.call-test has :cljd gating or runs on Dart. And whether `yin.vm.test-utils` is :cljd-clean. Let me check if cljd-out exists for it from previous full runs... this worktree is fresh. Let me grep for :cljd gates in test_utils / ast_walker / debruijn stack/register.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"32b5af6196bb4f1a9187b924\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"4aa38f94-22b6-473f-a7d4-22b05a86d914\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:25:16.552Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":4044,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:25448:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_2026111900251813ae3f6eae7e4284\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"Wait, `grep -c` shows test_utils.cljc is MISSING from the output — the first line lists ast_walker but not test_utils... Actually the output starts with `src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:0` — test_utils.cljc is absent, meaning grep found no match and returned... no, -c prints 0 for each file. The output shows 5 files; test_utils is missing — maybe grep errored on it? Or the output got truncated. Anyway `yang.core.call-test` has no :cljd gates and uses test-utils + runners, and `test/yang/python/antlr/*_test.cljc` too — those run on Dart (python worktree lanes ran int_ops etc. on multiple hosts per memory). So test-utils IS portable across hosts including Dart.\\\\n\\\\nWhat about Jolt? yang.core.call-test is NOT in the J2 included list — so it's never run on Jolt. My new namespace WILL be on Jolt (I add it to the manifest). Do the runners (ast-walker, debruijn stack/register, test-utils' dao.stream.ringbuffer) work on Jolt? yin.vm.ast-walker-test IS in included (line 275). yin.vm.debruijn.stack-test, debruijn-register-code-test etc. are included. dao.stream.ringbuffer-test included (line 254). test-utils itself is a helper ns, not a test — fine as long as it loads. dao.stream.memory-log — dao.stream.journal uses...? memory-log-test isn't in the list but wait — line 250: \\\\\\\"dao.stream.memory-log-test\\\\\\\" YES included.\\\\n\\\\nOK so test-utils should load on Jolt. But — risk: J2 lane runs JOLT via its own classpath/source root. The safest approach for the Jolt lane: keep my namespace's heavy requires minimal, and structure execution tests so they can run on all hosts. But the ruling demands \\\\\\\"Run portable fixtures through walker, semantic, stack, and register execution where those existing routes support them, on the four host lanes.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nThe four runners approach from call_test uses: yin.vm.test-utils (tu/create-vm, tu/secret, tu/make-stream), yin.vm.ast-walker (vm-load? actually vm/eval), yin.vm.semantic, derive, debruijn stack/register. All included-adjacent on Jolt. I'll go with it.\\\\n\\\\nOne concern: `vm/eval` — call_test's `:ast-walker` runner: `(vm/eval (tu/create-vm opts) ast)`. vm/eval exists in yin.vm. OK.\\\\n\\\\nAlso `tu/make-stream` needs ringbuffer — on Jolt should work (ringbuffer-test is included).\\\\n\\\\nNow — the J2/Jolt lane: my namespace will be added to `included` and `bare`. Note: the J2 lane currently REFUSES at manifest because of the four unrelated unlisted namespaces. So my Jolt gate will ALSO refuse — recorded honestly. Hmm wait — will it? The r2 report says J2 refuses naming `yin.repl.php-parser-test`, `yin.vm.code-entry-test`, `yin.vm.machine-data-test`, `yin.vm.provenance-test`. Those still exist unlisted → my Jolt run will exit 1 at manifest before running anything. The ruling: \\\\\\\"J2's four-namespace manifest refusal MUST be resolved by its owner before four-host acceptance; zero Jolt children is not a pass.\\\\\\\" So I record the refusal. But — can I still get SOME Jolt evidence? The refusal happens in check-manifest! before lane work. No bypass permitted (\\\\\\\"The optional namespace selector cannot bypass this check\\\\\\\"). So Jolt = honest refusal record.\\\\n\\\\nNow — the callsite test on Jolt DID run in cc4 r1? No — r2's table says Jolt exit 1 at manifest, 0 children. So \\\\\\\"four-host\\\\\\\" for this round = JVM, Node, Dart + Jolt-refusal-record. The ruling's test family 1 says \\\\\\\"Assert the constructor row has the same ordered child-address vector on JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt\\\\\\\" — with Jolt blocked by the owner dependency, I record that honestly.\\\\n\\\\nNow, let me also confirm how `vm/eval` works and the semantic lane loading — I'll copy call_test's runners verbatim style.\\\\n\\\\nNext: understand `annotate-call-sites` (does it walk operand vectors in order? paths by index — order changes paths of later operands only).\\\\n\\\\nOne IMPORTANT question for my implementation: sort STABILITY across hosts for ties (equal [k-root v-root]). sort-by in Clojure is stable (TimSort). ClojureScript's sort: `sort` calls `garray/stableSort` — stable. ClojureDart: sort uses... let me check ClojureDart's sort implementation — it's in clojure.core dart implementation. ClojureDart's `sort` uses `List.sort` with a comparison — Dart's List.sort is NOT guaranteed stable! Actually Dart's sort implementation (dual-pivot quicksort-ish) is unstable. Hmm — ClojureDart might implement stable sort itself. Let me check the vendored clojure.core cljd source... This matters only for ties: identical [k-root v-root] pairs — identical compiled content. As analyzed, tied pairs' permutation doesn't change canonical rows or sorted key vectors; the C1 occurrence-key vector is sorted canonically before digesting; per-position marker entries [origin root path :yang/call-site 1] — tied pairs swap between positions p and p' — the SET of marked entries is unchanged (same roots at same paths? wait — path p would then hold the OTHER AST object, but both objects have the SAME projected row id (same content) — so the occurrence [root path] entries are identical as data. The frontend metadata from reader positions could differ (different :line on each) — but position rows are [origin root path k v] with different values — those WOULD swap between hosts... but they're not part of C1/keys digests (only :yang/call-site filtered). BUT my family-2 snapshot includes full C1/keys + provenance — call-provenance is derived from the semantic vector — tied operands produce identical instructions at both positions, so instruction provenance identical. However `expansion-log`/full frontend-metadata comparisons aren't in my permutation snapshot. To be safe, my family-2 corpus should include ties but ALSO assert digests that don't depend on per-node metadata. Permutation entries share the same source forms anyway (same metadata on same forms across permutations... actually permutations permute the ORDER of the same form objects; with-meta forms keep their metadata — the metadata side table is keyed by path; a permuted map puts form A at path 3 vs path 5 — the [origin root path :line ...] rows DIFFER between permutations! So full frontend-metadata comparison across permutations is NOT invariant (positions differ) — that's why the cohort compares only C1 markers + provenance. I'll compare root + C1/keys digests + provenance digest (metadata-insensitive except call-site entries which are path-keyed but content-identical). \\\\n\\\\nHold on — are C1 marker entries path-dependent across permutations? Marker entry = [origin root path :yang/call-site 1]. Permutation P1: pair X at path [3 1], pair Y at [3 3]. P2: Y at [3 1], X at [3 3]. The SET of entries: {[o r [3 1] ...1], [o r [3 3] ...1]} — SAME set (values identical, roots identical since tree root is same and the occurrence content is the same). Yes — the sorted C1 relation is permutation-invariant. \\\\n\\\\nAnd the KEYS digest (marked rows, [origin root path]) — same set. Provenance: instruction call-provenance per application occurrence — tied/permuted pairs yield same instruction content at swapped positions; call-provenance records provenance per instruction... includes paths? If provenance keys include occurrence paths, the multiset is still identical (same set of [path, app-content] pairs across permutations? P1: [3 1]→X-content; P2: [3 1]→Y-content. If X≠Y (non-tied), the path→content mapping DIFFERS! Wait no — non-tied pairs get canonically SORTED, so after the fix both permutations emit the SAME operand order → identical path→content mapping. Only TIED pairs can differ, and tied = identical content → identical mapping. Phew. So post-fix, permutations yield byte-identical everything except reader-position metadata rows. Good.\\\\n\\\\nThe call-provenance: derived from linearize with frontend metadata? In replay-snapshot: `(linearize/project-rows tree nil rows)` where rows = frontend metadata — provenance relates instruction addresses to occurrence paths... Fine — post-fix identical.\\\\n\\\\nNow the critical question about permutation INVARIANCE of what I compare: I'll compare `snapshot` = root + keys-digest + c1-digest + instruction-provenance-digest (cohort model) — all permutation-invariant post-fix. RED pre-fix: permutations of insertion order → different host-map iteration → different operand order → different root. But CAREFUL: on the JVM with ≤8 entries, array-map preserves insertion; for permutations enumerated for sizes 0-4 (≤24 entries each)... sizes 0-4 = up to 4! = 24 permutations — array-map (≤8 entries = insertion-ordered). So two different insertion orders give different iteration orders → different operand vectors → different roots (unless the sort... there's no sort pre-fix). So pre-fix RED: at least two permutations of a ≥2-entry dynamic map differ in root. ✓. Post-fix all equal. ✓.\\\\n\\\\nRepresentation crossing: array-map vs hash-map for sizes 8/9/16/32 — hash-map iteration order differs from insertion; post-fix invariant regardless. Pre-fix RED via hash-map vs array-map same insertion → likely different order (JVM hashing)... For the RED proof I just need SOME inequality pre-fix; the 0-4 permutation case suffices.\\\\n\\\\nNow — an important question: how do I CONSTRUCT these maps as forms to compile? `(yang/compile form)` where form is a host map value. For \\\\\\\"insertion permutations\\\\\\\", build `(apply array-map k1 v1 k2 v2 ...)` in each permutation order; for hash-map representation `(apply hash-map ...)` (unordered arg order still defines insertion for hashing). Note hash-map with >8 entries on JVM; array-map beyond 8 entries on JVM *degrades* to hash-map automatically? `(apply array-map ...)` with >8 entries stays an array-map? In Clojure, array-map constructor always returns array-map (no promotion). Yes, `array-map` always returns PersistentArrayMap regardless of size.\\\\n\\\\nOn cljs: array-map → persistent array map, same. On cljd (Dart): array-map/hash-map exist? ClojureDart implements array-map and hash-map... The test must be portable — the ruling says \\\\\\\"available ordinary array-map/hash-map representations\\\\\\\" — use them but the corpus must work on all hosts. ClojureDart supports array-map/hash-map fns (they're in clojure.core). I'll use them.\\\\n\\\\nFloats: include 1.5, floats as keys/values.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\"quoted collection keys\\\\\\\": `'(quote [1 2])` — a quoted form as key. When compiled standalone, `(quote [1 2])` inside a collection is NOT constant-per-constant-form? (seq? form) top-level?=false → `constant-form?` for seq = top-level? && quote — nested quote inside a collection: `(every? #(constant-form? % false) form)` for the map — entry key `'(quote [1 2])` is a seq (quote [1 2]) with top-level?=false → false → dynamic → the whole map is constructed, and the key compiles via compile-form → compile-quote → literal. Good: quoted collection keys reach the emitter as compiled literal children.\\\\n\\\\nNested maps/sets/vectors as VALUES with dynamic content — recursion covers.\\\\n\\\\nNow — TEST FAMILY 5's \\\\\\\"no duplication\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"set-value collisions\\\\\\\": `(let [x 2] #{x 2})` → constructed set with elements [x, 2] → evaluates to #{2}. Element occurrences preserved pre-runtime: assert the compiled constructor has 2 element operands + seed even though both evaluate to 2 (runtime dedup is the set's job).\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\"Explicit assoc/conj calls, dynamic vectors, let, and do MUST retain written order; swapping their effectful arguments MUST remain observable and their ordered canonical rows MUST remain distinct\\\\\\\": compile `(assoc {} (tap :k1) (tap :v1) (tap :k2) (tap :v2))` vs swapped → different roots, and runtime trace follows written order. Dynamic vectors `[a b]` keep order (constructor vector branch). let/do order.\\\\n\\\\nNow — about the trace primitive on 4 lanes: I'll register a host module 'ord with op 'tap. Host module registration: `module/register-host-module registry name fns profiles`. Variable name to call: `ord/tap`? clojure_test's probe/note pattern: `(host 'probe [['note fn profile]])` then variables named `probe/note`. But those run under the convention lane (native + modules). For NATIVE compile (no convention), `(ord/tap :k1)` compiles as an ordinary application of variable `ord/tap` — resolved via module registry at runtime. The walkers resolve namespaced symbols through modules? In clojure_test, run-profile-outcome rewrites to `probe/note` variable in an outcome program (convention). For native... let me check how module-qualified variables resolve in the walker — get-variable: primitives/modules lookup by full symbol. I think `module/resolve` handles 'mod/op names. The stream tests: `(dao.stream.apply/call ...)` compiles to a dedicated node, not variable. Hmm.\\\\n\\\\nLet me find a native test calling a module-qualified fn... `cell/get` appears in unit-opts programs (u/sexp->uast '(fn ... (cell/get ...))). And clojure_test line 2239+: `{:bridge {:op/tick #(swap! n inc)}}` — a BRIDGE option, not module. Hmm, what's :bridge? Let me look at that test (stream ops). Actually simpler: avoid module-qualified names — bind the tap function in the ENVIRONMENT: `(tu/create-vm (merge opts {:env {'tap (fn [x] ...)}}))`. Do host fns in env work across the four runners? The walkers look up free variables in env. Host function values — callable? `vm/primitives` values are host fns and get called, so host fn application is supported generally. If env-supplied fns are treated as ordinary values, application of a non-fn would error... Let me check how application dispatches on host fns — search \\\\\\\"fn?\\\\\\\" in semantic/ast_walker.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"4110364c75054786875083ed\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"f3b35c91-cea5-4fe4-ac40-a98a4a623e84\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:26:16.618Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":56624,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:29483:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_2026111900303541b2e2a4d94644ec\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"Rich data. Key observations:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Anonymous arity map** (even WITHOUT explicit source meta): has FOUR pairs — reason/arity, argc/count, kind/anonymous, source/{:line 46, :column 39}. Interesting — `:yin.k/source` is present even for `'(fn [x] x)` with no meta: line 46 column 39 — that's the position of... my diagnostic file's fn form? The forms were constructed by the test compile — `(quote (fn [x] x))` — no meta. Where does {:line 46 :column 39} come from? That's compile-program attaching?? Hmm — `source-meta` reads `(meta form)` — for a plain quoted fn form there's no meta... unless the READER of the test file attached position metadata to the symbol? No wait — this is the arity map's :yin.k/source with {:line 46 :column 39} — my diag file line 46! The `deftest diag-anon-test` — line 46 is where `(quote (fn [x] x))` sits in fixorder_tmp_diag_test.cljc. Clojure's reader attaches :line/:column meta to lists (seqs) read from files! So `(quote (fn [x] x))` carries reader position meta from MY test file. So anon arity maps DO carry :yin.k/source with reader positions when the reader supplied them.\\\\n\\\\nSo the anonymous arity map = 4 pairs. Key roots: reason 81b62c1e, argc 8930bf5e, kind 476c5dac, source 58a8f530. Sorted pair-key order by first element (hex string compare): 476c5dac (kind) < 58a8f530 (source) < 81b62c1e (reason) < 8930bf5e (argc)? Compare \\\\\\\"47...\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\"58...\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\"81...\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\"89...\\\\\\\" yes. So canonical order: kind, source, reason, argc.\\\\n\\\\n2. **`pair-sorted?` false** for pre-fix anon/named/lower-unit arity maps — RED oracle confirmed.\\\\n\\\\n3. `lower-unit` shows arity maps for `identity` (library fn!) and `replay` — the `library-for` adapters also generate arity maps (identity). Named `identity` arity map: same key order — fn pair sorts first post-fix.\\\\n\\\\n4. The seed root 201f235c… is the `{}` literal root — stable across all constructors.\\\\n\\\\nNote: the anon map's `:yin.k/source` VALUE root differs between the no-source variant (59f93b7e = {:line 46 :column 39}) and with-source (fb4a46fd = {:line 7 :column 2}). Both fine.\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT design consequence: since reader metadata attaches positions to forms from TEST SOURCE files, my permutation tests must construct forms in ways with CONTROLLED metadata — permutations built at runtime via `apply array-map` produce fresh maps (the map itself has no meta; keys are quoted literals from my file → carry file positions, but PERMUTATIONS of the same entry objects keep each entry's metadata identical — so cross-permutation comparison is fine: same objects, same metadata, only insertion order differs. The compiled AST nodes' metadata merge from the form — same per entry regardless of position. Good.\\\\n\\\\nBUT careful for family 4's \\\\\\\"changing only source-position metadata MUST NOT change code order/root\\\\\\\": compare form without meta vs with-meta positions — root must be equal. Since compile-form-1 merges meta onto the AST NODE (vary-meta) and projection drops node meta — root unchanged. ✓.\\\\n\\\\nNow — the pair-sorted? oracle function in my diag had a bug risk: `(partition 2 (rest roots))` pairs must be [k v]; comparing consecutive pair VECTORS via canonical-compare — my diag code: `(partition 2 1 (partition 2 (rest roots)))` gives pairs of pair-vectors; `(vec a)` — partition gives lists, I vec'd them. Result `false` = not sorted pre-fix. \\\\n\\\\nAlso important: does `jing/canonical-compare` handle vectors of KEYWORDS? Yes — canonical CBOR of vectors of keywords.\\\\n\\\\nNow, an important question about **Jing canonical CBOR and the \\\\\\\":segment/blake3-…\\\\\\\" keyword vector comparison\\\\\\\": keyword with namespace — CBOR text encoding \\\\\\\"segment/blake3-201f…\\\\\\\"? The compare is bytewise on the encoded form — deterministic. Fine.\\\\n\\\\nNext: what about **`arity-error-form` as covered producer**: the ruling says arity-error-form is covered, not a second authority — no edit needed there. ✓.\\\\n\\\\nNow let me consider **how `sort-by` handles the comparator across hosts** and write the implementation. Also double-check ClojureDart's `sort-by` availability. clojure.core/sort-by exists in ClojureDart. Its stability: irrelevant for content (ties identical). BUT — one more consideration: ClojureDart sort comparator protocol expects negative/zero/positive — canonical-compare returns exactly that. ✓.\\\\n\\\\nAlso `sort-by` returns a lazy? In Clojure `sort-by` returns a sorted seq (not lazy). `vec` wrap.\\\\n\\\\nOne performance consideration: `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` per child — each call re-projects the whole subtree. For a map with N children each of depth d — fine.\\\\n\\\\nEDGE CASES to consider in the emitter rewrite:\\\\n\\\\n1. **`compile-form` recursion for children** — keep exactly `(compile-form % false env macro-env)`.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Sets**: `(vec form)` — set iteration order is host-dependent; sort by element root: single-element sort key `[root]`.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Vectors**: untouched — `(vec form)` order preserved.\\\\n\\\\n4. **Empty maps/sets**: `constant-form?` returns true for empty (every? on empty = true) → literal path, never reaches the emitter. Non-issue but seed still emitted for non-empty.\\\\n\\\\n5. **Metadata on the FORM itself**: old code — `compile-form-1` merges form meta onto the returned application node AFTER compile-form* returns. My rewrite returns the call map; compile-form-1 still merges. ✓ unchanged behavior.\\\\n\\\\n6. **compute each child once**: I compile each key/value once. The sort must not recompile — I keep compiled ASTs. ✓.\\\\n\\\\n7. **The seed**: unchanged `{:type :literal, :value seed}` first. ✓.\\\\n\\\\n8. **`tail?`**: unchanged via cond->. ✓.\\\\n\\\\n9. **What if a child AST projection throws \\\\\\\"Unknown AST node type\\\\\\\"?** Children are compile-form products — all projectable (validate-ast-call-sites! already projects final trees). BUT — wait: is that true for EVERY child? The final tree gets annotated and validated by C1 validation... is validate-ast-call-sites! ALWAYS run? compile-form runs `annotate-call-sites` — does annotation validate? Not necessarily projection. But encoder/project runs on publication. Hmm — are there compile-form products that can't project? `:dao.stream.apply/call` — in the grammar? The callsite test marks it as non-application node kind, and vm/occurrences handles it... `semantic-bytecode-grammar` — let me check it includes :dao.stream.apply/call, :stream/make, :vm/current-continuation, :yin/def... Let me look at the full grammar table to be safe.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"facfa2180106445dbfc72549\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"925903fd-5567-429c-8212-4cab65fe8143\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:31:08.090Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":27713,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:29486:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_e757d895b889467bb2b0b01f\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"1035\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\n1036\\\\t;; docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md §2. One row `[id tag & slots]` per\\\\n1037\\\\t;; distinct node; `id` is `(jing/segment-key [tag & slots])`.\\\\n1038\\\\t\\\\n1039\\\\t(def semantic-bytecode-grammar\\\\n1040\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The Universal AST row-slot table (yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md §2.3):\\\\n1041\\\\t   tag -> ordered `[field kind]` slots, rather than executable bytecode. Position `i` of a\\\\n1042\\\\t   row body (after the tag) holds the map field named at `i`, so this one\\\\n1043\\\\t   table drives both projection and reconstruction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n1044\\\\t  {:literal [[:value :data]],\\\\n1045\\\\t   :variable [[:name :sym]],\\\\n1046\\\\t   :lambda [[:params :syms] [:body :node]],\\\\n1047\\\\t   :application [[:operator :node] [:operands :nodes] [:tail? :bool]],\\\\n1048\\\\t   :if [[:test :node] [:consequent :node] [:alternate :node]],\\\\n1049\\\\t   :dao.stream.apply/call [[:op :kw] [:operands :nodes]],\\\\n1050\\\\t   :vm/gensym [[:prefix :str]],\\\\n1051\\\\t   :vm/store-get [[:key :key]],\\\\n1052\\\\t   :vm/store-put [[:key :key] [:val :data]],\\\\n1053\\\\t   :vm/current-continuation [],\\\\n1054\\\\t   :vm/park [],\\\\n1055\\\\t   :vm/resume [[:parked-id :kw] [:val :node]],\\\\n1056\\\\t   :stream/make [[:buffer :int]],\\\\n1057\\\\t   :stream/put [[:target :node] [:val :node]],\\\\n1058\\\\t   :stream/cursor [[:source :node]],\\\\n1059\\\\t   :stream/next [[:source :node]],\\\\n1060\\\\t   :stream/close [[:source :node]]})\\\\n1061\\\\t\\\\n1062\\\\t\\\\n1063\\\\t(def ^:private semantic-bytecode-defaults\\\\n1064\\\\t  \\\\\\\"§2.4 saturation: `[tag field]` -> the default written map→rows. rows→map\\\\n1065\\\\t   never re-defaults; a nil in one of these slots is a defect.\\\\\\\"\\\\n1066\\\\t  {[:vm/gensym :prefix] \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\",\\\\n1067\\\\t   [:stream/make :buffer] default-stream-capacity,\\\\n1068\\\\t   [:application :tail?] false,\\\\n1069\\\\t   [:application :operands] [],\\\\n1070\\\\t   [:dao.stream.apply/call :operands] []})\\\\n1071\\\\t\\\\n1072\\\\t\\\\n1073\\\\t(defn- strip-symbol-meta\\\\n1074\\\\t  \\\\\\\"§2.5: reader metadata on names is frontend metadata, not content.\\\\\\\"\\\\n1075\\\\t  [x]\\\\n1076\\\\t  (if (symbol? x) (with-meta x nil) x))\\\\n1077\\\\t\\\\n1078\\\\t\\\\n1079\\\\t(defn- strip-reader-positions\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"4bf0778c-5ffe-491f-9260-243172edaa1a\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:31:08.414Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"file\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\";; =============================================================================\\\\n;; docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md §2. One row `[id tag & slots]` per\\\\n;; distinct node; `id` is `(jing/segment-key [tag & slots])`.\\\\n\\\\n(def semantic-bytecode-grammar\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The Universal AST row-slot table (yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md §2.3):\\\\n   tag -> ordered `[field kind]` slots, rather than executable bytecode. Position `i` of a\\\\n   row body (after the tag) holds the map field named at `i`, so this one\\\\n   table drives both projection and reconstruction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  {:literal [[:value :data]],\\\\n   :variable [[:name :sym]],\\\\n   :lambda [[:params :syms] [:body :node]],\\\\n   :application [[:operator :node] [:operands :nodes] [:tail? :bool]],\\\\n   :if [[:test :node] [:consequent :node] [:alternate :node]],\\\\n   :dao.stream.apply/call [[:op :kw] [:operands :nodes]],\\\\n   :vm/gensym [[:prefix :str]],\\\\n   :vm/store-get [[:key :key]],\\\\n   :vm/store-put [[:key :key] [:val :data]],\\\\n   :vm/current-continuation [],\\\\n   :vm/park [],\\\\n   :vm/resume [[:parked-id :kw] [:val :node]],\\\\n   :stream/make [[:buffer :int]],\\\\n   :stream/put [[:target :node] [:val :node]],\\\\n   :stream/cursor [[:source :node]],\\\\n   :stream/next [[:source :node]],\\\\n   :stream/close [[:source :node]]})\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(def ^:private semantic-bytecode-defaults\\\\n  \\\\\\\"§2.4 saturation: `[tag field]` -> the default written map→rows. rows→map\\\\n   never re-defaults; a nil in one of these slots is a defect.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  {[:vm/gensym :prefix] \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\",\\\\n   [:stream/make :buffer] default-stream-capacity,\\\\n   [:application :tail?] false,\\\\n   [:application :operands] [],\\\\n   [:dao.stream.apply/call :operands] []})\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- strip-symbol-meta\\\\n  \\\\\\\"§2.5: reader metadata on names is frontend metadata, not content.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  [x]\\\\n  (if (symbol? x) (with-meta x nil) x))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- strip-reader-positions\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":45,\\\"startLine\\\":1035,\\\"totalLines\\\":2202}},\\\"tool_result_meta\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_e757d895b889467bb2b0b01f\\\",\\\"permission_decision\\\":{\\\"decision\\\":\\\"accept\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"config\\\",\\\"reason_type\\\":\\\"mode\\\"}}]}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:29601:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_202611190031092f0f2cd389654a58\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"The grammar covers all node kinds compile-form produces (`:literal`, `:variable`, `:lambda`, `:application`, `:if`, `:dao.stream.apply/call`, stream nodes via special forms?). One caveat: children with `:yang/call-site` marker keys — projection drops `:yang/*` keys (per docstring). During nested compilation markers aren't yet attached anyway.\\\\n\\\\nOne more: `compile-form` can return nodes of type... let me confirm there are no other :type values in yang/clojure.cljc emit paths:\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"cd69aa2f12ef4d40a7c48a1b\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"f853f4cf-5a66-4513-8378-da9c855cc8c9\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:31:14.608Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":1619,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:29603:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_b5905b472f6948f08609de28\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"  17 :type :application\\\\n   2 :type :cell-ref\\\\n   2 :type :dao.stream.apply/call\\\\n   2 :type :if\\\\n  10 :type :lambda\\\\n  11 :type :literal\\\\n   1 :type :user\\\\n  13 :type :variable\\\",\\\"is_error\\\":false}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"bede7f4d-6886-4a54-a18f-2d24a406e235\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:31:15.176Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"  17 :type :application\\\\n   2 :type :cell-ref\\\\n   2 :type :dao.stream.apply/call\\\\n   2 :type :if\\\\n  10 :type :lambda\\\\n  11 :type :literal\\\\n   1 :type :user\\\\n  13 :type :variable\\\",\\\"stderr\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"interrupted\\\":false,\\\"isImage\\\":false,\\\"noOutputExpected\\\":false},\\\"tool_result_meta\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_b5905b472f6948f08609de28\\\",\\\"permission_decision\\\":{\\\"decision\\\":\\\"accept\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"config\\\",\\\"reason_type\\\":\\\"subcommandResults\\\"}}]}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:31786:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_2026111900312370e068f460a24229\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"The `:cell-ref` matches in clojure.cljc:1362 are `(u/lit :cell-ref)` — a literal whose VALUE is the keyword :cell-ref — not a node type. And the others are inside quoted sexp data. So all emitted NODE types are :application/:dao.stream.apply/call/:if/:lambda/:literal/:variable — all projectable.\\\\n\\\\nWait, the grep counted `:type :cell-ref` twice though... line 1347 is a comment; the `:type :cell-ref` grep hits at 2307/2332 are comments/data inside quoted forms? Line 2331 `(dsg__get dsg__candidate :type)` — the grep pattern `:type :cell-ref` matched line 2332? That's `:cell-ref)` in `(dsg__equal (dsg__get dsg__candidate :type) :cell-ref)`. OK — comments/data only. Safe.\\\\n\\\\nNow — what about `u/sexp->uast`-generated maps INSIDE compile-program's convention preamble (the `dsg__` forms)? Those are quoted sexps compiled via `u/sexp->uast` — which \\\\\\\"treats its non-list collection values as literals\\\\\\\" (ruling RULING 2) — not via my emitter. Fine, untouched.\\\\n\\\\nCRITICAL next question: where else does the argument-vector path build HOST MAPS that reach compile-form? E.g. `compile-av-fn` descriptor maps — `{:yin.k/arities ...}` as constructed maps? From the diagnostics, the arity maps are the visible multi-pair cases. Others (yang.call maps) come through `u/sexp->uast` (literals — untouched).\\\\n\\\\nNow — design the **implementation**. Rewrite `compile-constructed-collection` + add private helper `content-root`:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(defn- compiled-root\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The canonical semantic-bytecode root of one compiled child: the content\\\\n   address of its projected executable content, the sort key constructed\\\\n   collections order their operands by (constructed collections v1).\\\\\\\"\\\\n  [ast]\\\\n  (:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- compile-constructed-collection\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Lower a collection literal with dynamic elements to constructor calls over\\\\n   existing portable primitives, so unquoted vectors/maps/sets evaluate their\\\\n   elements like Clojure. Constant collections stay :literal nodes.\\\\n\\\\n   [e1 e2 ...]  => (conj [] e1 e2 ...)\\\\n   #{e1 e2 ...} => (conj #{} e1 e2 ...)\\\\n   {k1 v1 ...}  => (assoc {} k1 v1 k2 v2 ...)\\\\n\\\\n   A host map or set has no portable iteration order, so a map's pairs and a\\\\n   set's elements enter the operand vector in compiled-content order\\\\n   (constructed collections v1): each child compiles once, and the pairs\\\\n   (element records) sort ascending by jing/canonical-compare over the\\\\n   children's projected semantic-bytecode roots — a map pair by the vector\\\\n   [key-root value-root], a set element by its own root — after the unchanged\\\\n   seed operand and before the unchanged operator resolution and tail mark.\\\\n   The sort keys are temporary compile-time data: nothing is published, and\\\\n   no child is recompiled, deduplicated or evaluated. A vector keeps its\\\\n   written order (the control), and equal sort keys are equal executable\\\\n   content, so a tie needs no further tie-break.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  [form tail? env macro-env]\\\\n  (let [ctor (cond\\\\n               (vector? form) ['conj []]\\\\n               (set? form) ['conj #{}]\\\\n               :else ['assoc {}])\\\\n        [op seed] ctor\\\\n        compile-child #(compile-form % false env macro-env)\\\\n        ;; pairs/elements sorted by compiled content; each child compiled once\\\\n        sorted-children\\\\n        (cond\\\\n          (vector? form) {:order :written, :children (mapv compile-child form)}\\\\n          (map? form)\\\\n          (->> (map (fn [k v]\\\\n                      (let [ck (compile-child k)\\\\n                            cv (compile-child v)]\\\\n                        [(compiled-root ck) (compiled-root cv) ck cv]))\\\\n                    (keys form) (vals form))\\\\n               (sort-by (fn [[kr vr]] [kr vr]) jing/canonical-compare)\\\\n               ...)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nHmm — sort-by keyfn destructuring. Cleaner:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(map (fn [k v]\\\\n       (let [ck (compile-child k), cv (compile-child v)]\\\\n         {:sort-key [(compiled-root ck) (compiled-root cv)], :asts [ck cv]}))\\\\n     (keys form) (vals form))\\\\n(sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare)\\\\n(mapcat :asts)\\\\nvec\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nFor sets:\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(map (fn [e]\\\\n       (let [ce (compile-child e)]\\\\n         {:sort-key [(compiled-root ce)], :asts [ce]}))\\\\n     form)\\\\n(sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare)\\\\n(mapcat :asts)\\\\nvec\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nThen:\\\\n```clojure\\\\ncall {:type :application,\\\\n      :operator {:type :variable, :name op},\\\\n      :operands (into [{:type :literal, :value seed}] children)}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nWait — for the vector branch old code did `(map #(compile-form % false env macro-env) args)` where args=(vec form) — same.\\\\n\\\\nNote on `(map f (keys form) (vals form))` — multiple-collection map stops at shortest; keys/vals same length. ✓ And this compiles keys+values ONCE each. ✓\\\\n\\\\nThe comparator: `(sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare records)` — sort-by passes `(comparator (keyfn a) (keyfn b))` → canonical-compare on the two key VECTORS. Ruling: \\\\\\\"The concrete key is the vector of two address values\\\\\\\". ✓ Set: 1-element vector — fine (\\\\\\\"each compiled element's canonical UAST root as the sort key\\\\\\\" — a 1-vector wrapping is an implementation detail; canonical-compare over [r] vs [r'] equals compare of r vs r' since CBOR array-of-1 prefixes are identical). Good.\\\\n\\\\nOne subtlety — `sort-by` on CLJS with a keyword keyfn: fine.\\\\n\\\\nAlso `mapcat :asts` — transducer-less; fine.\\\\n\\\\nDocstring: bounds say \\\\\\\"precise docstrings\\\\\\\" allowed. Good.\\\\n\\\\nNow — is there any OTHER place that emits constructed collections from HOST traversal? RULING 2 scope: \\\\\\\"all dynamic/unquoted map and set forms reaching compile-constructed-collection\\\\\\\". Only the one emitter. ✓\\\\n\\\\n**Manifest** (frontends.cljc): revision \\\\\\\"c1-2\\\\\\\", add `:yang.frontend/lowering-profile :yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`. Keep support-profile :yang.clojure/c1. Docstring updated.\\\\n\\\\nNow the TESTS. Let me design the full test namespace now. Requirements traceability (families 1-7). I'll write it, run RED, implement, iterate to GREEN, then record fixtures/expectations (the recorded digests need post-fix recording — so I'll put placeholders first, run RED for order tests, implement, then record).\\\\n\\\\nPlan the namespace skeleton:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(ns yang.clojure-collection-order-test\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Constructed collections v1 (ruling 1795017757000): the canonical\\\\n   compiled-content operand ordering of dynamic map/set literals at\\\\n   yang.clojure's collection emitter — RULING 1 order, RULING 2 semantics,\\\\n   RULING 3 tests 1-7, RULING 4 manifest/legacy boundaries.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\\\n            [clojure.walk :as walk]\\\\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\\\\n            [yang.clojure :as yang]\\\\n            [yang.core.uast :as u]\\\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\\\\n            [yin.vm.data :as data]\\\\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\\\\n            [yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]\\\\n            [yin.vm.macro :as macro]\\\\n            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\\\\n            [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\\\\n            [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as linearize]\\\\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\\\\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\\\\n            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]\\\\n            #?@(:clj [[clojure.edn :as edn] [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]])))\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nWait — reading the legacy fixture: fixture is EDN — reading EDN portably: clojure.edn exists on cljs (cljs.reader/read-string needed) and cljd... EDN reading on Dart — cljs.tools.reader? For portability, better: store the legacy fixture as EDN and read with a host-appropriate reader — cljs has cljs.reader/read-string (require [cljs.reader]); cljd — does clojure.edn exist in ClojureDart? ClojureDart provides clojure.edn? I believe ClojureDart implements clojure.edn/read-string. Risky. Alternative: store the fixture as CBOR (dao.jing.cbor decode — portable everywhere, used by dao.stream.cbor-test on all hosts). The cc4-replay-jvm.cbor fixture was written via cbor encode. And keywords in CBOR decode fine. Let me check dao.jing.cbor API: encode/decode. The fixture would be a map {:root ... :rows {...} :result ...} encoded. Reading: `(cbor/decode bytes)` — bytes from where? Reading a resource file portably: io/resource on cljs needs shadow-cljs :resource paths... How do other portable tests read fixtures? Let me check how tests read resource files portably — search io/resource in cljc tests.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"726deadfb910476aa6c3a449\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"1122b225-2e52-48de-b87e-ef0ed2c87915\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-18T16:31:56.417Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":28329,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795018441000-engineer-fix-constructed-collection-order-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:31787:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_2026111900312370e068f460a24229\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_0223d6e28efb4403882ace69\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Bash\\\",\\\"inpu