ity, :yin.k/argc ..., :yin.k/fn ..., :yin.k/kind ..., :yin.k/source ...} — insertion order fixed. Post-fix irrelevant. ✓\\\\n\\\\nAnother subtlety — `compile-av-fn` may build maps with MORE structure — fine.\\\\n\\\\nNow the LEGACY fixture capture. Also the RECORDING of digests. Order of work (TDD):\\\\n\\\\n1. Write capture namespace for the legacy pre-fix fixture (runs NOW at pre-fix compiler) → writes CBOR + prints old root/value. **Must run BEFORE implementing the fix.**\\\\n2. Write the full test namespace with all expectations except the post-fix recorded digests (family 1d/recorded child-address vector — those are fix-invariant! I already HAVE them from the diagnostics: the operand-root vector post-fix = [201f235c, 1e03667e, 3156a070, 81b62c1e, 1db5dbd0, 8930bf5e, e60543d2] — recorded NOW, valid post-fix). The whole-tree digests for members — need post-fix recording (placeholder markers, filled after implementation).\\\\n3. Run new namespace → RED (order tests fail; permutation tests fail).\\\\n4. Implement emitter + manifest.\\\\n5. Run → mostly green; record whole-tree digests; fill in; re-run green.\\\\n6. Callsite cohort: post-fix digests differ → update replay-expectations + snapshots resource (archive old under pre-fix name); run JVM/Node/Dart gates.\\\\n7. Focused additions to clojure_callsite_test.cljc (family coverage for cohort-adjacent assertions): the ruling says \\\\\\\"add focused regression assertions and replace active expectations only through the controlled re-pin\\\\\\\". I'll add a focused deftest asserting the arity-map constructor order + a comment documenting re-pin.\\\\n8. J2 manifest additions; run Jolt lane (expected refusal — record).\\\\n9. kondo on touched files; ledger; findings.\\\\n\\\\nThe legacy fixture capture — write it now. Contents:\\\\n- program form P = `((fn [x] {(- x 1) :b (+ x 1) :a}) 1)` (old JVM insertion order (- x 1) first).\\\\n- rows = (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode (yang/compile P)) → {:root :rows}.\\\\n- env execution result: run with env {'1...} wait — `(fn [x] ...)` applied to 1 — x bound by lambda. Runner: walker (vm/eval) with primitives assoc. Value = {2 :a} pre-fix.\\\\n- also record `:entry-map` the OLD operand-root vector for the constructor (evidence of old order) and expected post-fix difference.\\\\n\\\\nCBOR encode of {:root kw :rows {kw [...]}} — jing/canonical-bytes (cbor/encode). Write to test/resources/yang/clojure/cc-collection-order-legacy-prefix.cbor. Hmm — name per ruling: \\\\\\\"Versioned post-fix captures/expectations and historical copies needed to retain pre-fix evidence\\\\\\\" — fixture name like `constructed-collection-order-legacy-c1-1.cbor`.\\\\n\\\\nWait — CBOR profile: rows are `[kw :application kw [kw] false]` — vectors with keywords/symbols?? Rows contain TAGS as KEYWORDS (:application) — CBOR profile supports keywords ✓. Symbols? rows contain no symbols... :variable rows contain symbols ('assoc). `:sym` slots in rows hold SYMBOLS (e.g. row [id :variable assoc]). CBOR encode of symbols — the Jing profile supports symbols? canonical-bytes hashes ASTs containing symbols... dao.jing.cbor supports symbols? The cc4-replay-jvm.cbor contains full trees incl. symbols — it was written with cbor encode ✓ (r2: \\\\\\\"Binary canonical CBOR retains even origins whose printed keyword cannot be re-read as EDN\\\\\\\"). OK symbols fine.\\\\n\\\\nEnv {'fn value}? No env needed.\\\\n\\\\nAlso record the RUNTIME VALUE {2 :a} — a plain map — CBOR ✓.\\\\n\\\\nExecution portability of the fixture loader: read BYTES per host: clj `(Files/readAllBytes (Path-of path))`? simpler: `(.readAllBytes (java.nio.file.Paths/get path (make-array String 0)))`. cljs fs.readFileSync → Buffer/Uint8Array ✓. cljd File.readAsBytesSync ✓. jolt → :clj branch (Paths) — jolt runs on its own host with java.nio available? Jolt is a Clojure-like on Chez? The jolt host... cbor_fixtures used slurp for :clj — plain slurp returns String (bad for bytes). For bytes on clj: use `(java.nio.file.Files/readAllBytes ...)`. Jolt: java interop?? Jolt is NOT JVM — `:clj` branch on jolt would break IF my namespace runs there. Hmm — jolt provides its own host IO? The jolt smoke used defcfn... Risky. Jolt lane can't run anyway until the manifest owner unblocks (recorded refusal), but my namespace should still LOAD on jolt eventually. I'll gate the file-reading part: make the legacy test read bytes via a small helper with #?(:clj java.nio, :cljs fs, :cljd dart-io) — jolt takes :clj. If jolt lacks java.nio, the legacy test would fail there — record as known boundary in findings (jolt lane currently refuses at manifest anyway). Hmm — better: don't hard-require java.nio; on :clj slurping bytes: `(with-open [in (clojure.java.io/input-stream path)] ...)` — also JVM-specific. It's fine: jolt acceptance is already blocked by the owner dependency; I'll note the IO dependency honestly.\\\\n\\\\nLet me now check what a jolt-hosted clojure looks like — actually jolt_j2_lane runs test namespaces under `-M:jolt` with jolt binary — jolt implements enough clojure to run the suite. java.nio interop won't exist. To be maximally safe I could encode the fixture bytes as BASE64 TEXT inside an EDN/CLJC data literal (a def of a base64 string constant!) — no file IO at all! decode: dao.host has base64 (dao.stream.base64-test exists). `dao.jing.cbor` + base64 decode → bytes → cbor/decode. Base64 decode portable across ALL hosts including jolt, no IO. The fixture becomes a tracked source file with a giant base64 string — acceptable (few KB).\\\\n\\\\nBase64 decode helper: dao.stream.base64? or dao.host? Let me check what's available: dao.stream.base64-test is in J2 list... find the codec ns.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"3566f1b970ff4bbd88f5ffee\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\\\",\\\